A||||32||newfie||2.8L turbo on a 3.4L||||1||
Z||000000||newfie||11-20-2001||09:36 AM||||Will a 2.8L turbo kit like to one at design systems fit on the 3.4L pushrod?  What are the major differences between the 2.8 and 3.4, What's the same?  I know the intake off the fiero fits the 3.4l, how bout the exhuast and everything else?||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||11-20-2001||09:50 AM||||yes everything will transfer over... but get with predator.. he's haveing lean issues.. and now rich issues. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Predator||11-20-2001||01:36 PM||||From what I know the kit should bolt right up to the 3.4. The turbo should be big enough, I have no noticeable lag myself. I have had a lot of problems with the new chip that came with the kit. Ed, at Performance who has been trying to tune my car has stated that he really likes the kit, but the computer settings are wrong, and have been a constant struggle to get right. I have spent a ton of money on trying to get the lean/rich condition out of the turbo system. The actual system is designed great according to Ed, but the chip is not programmed correctly so if you get a kit make sure you take it to a dyno, or you could be spending money to get new head gaskets like I did. I haven't heard anything back from Ed on a solution to my problem yet. He is trying to find something that can fix the now rich condition without having to buy a complete new computer control system for it. When and if I get the car running right I will post in detail all that has been done to get it so. Until then I would be leary of the Design One System kit. Others have posted that they have the kit without issues. My only question to them is have they had them dyno tested? If so it would be interesting to see the results to see if this is an isolated case, or a defect overall. I am currently running 169 hp and 212 ftlbs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||11-20-2001||09:25 PM||||Pedator, is there more power there? Can you feel it? Was it worth it? I know you haven't got it tuned right (Is there such a thing?), but if it was, how much more power do ya think will go to the wheels? And is that 169 hp and 212 ft. lbs. at the wheels?<P>If you were to get a new computer for that setup, what will it be?<P>And finally, can you give me the specs on that IHI turbo? What is the exhaust A/R and the compressor A/R?||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||11-21-2001||04:40 AM||||I know Predators car was run on a chassis dyno at Engineered, so the hp numbers are at the wheels.  While 169hp/212tq doesn't sound like a lot, consider the fact that the stock Fiero V6 dyno's at around 100hp to the wheels! So yeah, having approximately 70 extra hp at the wheels is a phenominal gain over a stock Fiero.  Also consider the fact that this is probably only around 7-10psi boost.  Imagine what you could do with 15psi boost, intercooler, bigger turbo, and a well-built V6!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Predator||11-21-2001||07:46 AM||||Like GT said it was run on a Dyno. That is how Ed is trying to tune it in. He expects to get some more torque out of it and maybe a few more ponies at lower RPM's. That is where I run rich now. Yes you can tell a big difference in the feel of the car. It really wants to go especially at highway speeds. The question of whether it was worth it is debatable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. In my case I would have done a few things different that would have saved me a ton of money to begin with. First, I would have bought the kit new instead of from ebay. I had to get the turbo rebuilt which ran around $1,000 for parts and labor. It is an expensive turbo, but Ed says it is a very good turbo. I would have had the car put on the dyno to begin with where I would have found that I had really dirty injectors and was running stupid lean. With the turbo on I blew a head gasket. That was another $1,400 dollars parts and labor. I bought the kit from ebay for $2,700 dollars so as you can see I have spent a lot more than I originally planned. Sometimes experience is expensive. Next thing I would have done was right after I installed the kit I would have put it on the dyno to have it tuned. I would have probably not even bothered with the chip sent from Design One, and bought an adjustable computer control system for Ed to manually tune the car in. It would have cost more, but with all the extra money I spent learning it would have more than paid for itself, and it looks like I may end up having to do that anyway to get the car right. The 169 212 is at the wheels, and that's not bad considering the rich condition. Of course 1ST2M6 runs better numbers than that without a turbo,<BR>and with his NOS he is off the chart compared to my setup, and spent a lot less to get there. I am running 9 - 10 lbs of boost which is all Ed recommends unless the bottom end is built up. The last thing I want is to grenade my engine after I have spent a small fortune getting it built. If you take your time and research all that needs to be done in depth you can do the turbo setup spending a lot less money than I did, and have a nice setup. I am not sure what the advantage of putting a bigger turbo on is unless you are running more boost. I guess that is my ignorance on turbos. To me 9 - 10 lbs from a small IHI is the same as 9 - 10 lbs from a T3 without the T3 lag.. To me running the smallest turbo you can is preferable to getting the biggest turbo you can to produce the same amount of boost. According to Ed the turbo I have is capable of producing up to around 14 lbs of boost on my engine. Anyway good luck if you decide to go the Turbo route.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||newfie||11-21-2001||12:00 PM||||What size turbo does design one use?  A T3?  What do you have to consider when sizing a turbo?  The 3.4L has more capacity than the stock 2.8L so can handle a bigger turbo.  How about the AR?  What would be a good value for the 3.4L with a intercooler. Want to run as much as 14lbs boost.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||lou_dias||11-21-2001||01:24 PM||||Wow, your 169hp vs. my 147 (maybe 150 since removing the SpiralMax, thanks Trav.) and the same amount of torque.<BR>I wonder if I can close the gap on your turbo once I put in my REAL ported and polished heads, ported TB and intake and use the stock camaro cam instead<p>[This message has been edited by lou_dias (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||aleroboy||12-12-2001||06:30 PM||||If you go turbo the later style head on the 3.4L are the best. the 3.4L that comes in my car produces 170hp and 200ft/lbs stock!<BR>when I start my fiero project this is the moor I want to use. I've found a place to do lower compression pistions for my motor.<P>also predator, if your interested I can talk to super chips about making a prom for your setup. custom chips are arround $200. if you have air flow charts from your dyno run this is all they need. I hope you get everythign working for you. I was thinking about starting with some parts from the design1 kit<BR>now i'm looking into the electromotive tech2 and upcomming tech3.<BR>I don't know much about the wireing in the car so  I was going to buy a kit from painless and start from scratch.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||12-12-2001||08:38 PM||||ok now this is just my opinion so take it as that.<P>The turbo IRM/Design one provides is an IHI turbo.  I have the part number of it around here somewhere and will try to find it so you guys can do some cross referencing.<P>however I feel that the IHI turbo is too small for anything larger then a 2.8.  Predator even said he noticed no lag.  Which means he probably has boost as low as 1800 rpm or just off idle.  Good for around town driving but bad when you get up around 6k rpm.  Way too much heat from that turbo!!!  So on anything bigger then a 2.8 it will produce even more heat then you want.  <P>To be honest with you even the t-3 from a thunderbird that everyone talks about is too small for anything bigger then a 2.8.  in fact I still think it is too small for even a 2.8.  I have plotted the curves on turbo maps and at anything over 9 psi on a stock 2.8 engine the turbo is just not efficient at all.  It runs at like 60% efficiency and spinning well over 100k rpms which is down right awefull if you ask me.  <P>So anything under 9psi on a 2.8 is should be relatively ok but still too small I think.  Anything larger then a 2.8 and you are asking for trouble.  <P>I think the 60-1 super turbo from Turbonetics is the way to go!  It produces boost at a very low rpm cause the way the turbine wheel is setup but it won't run out of steam till over 400 hp.  One can expect boost to come on with this turbo around 2200 rpm and have full boost by 3500 rpm set at 10 psi.  Efficiency with this turbo will be in the 70% range.  Intake temps will be much lower then with a t-3 with a 60-1 and your engine will appreciate that.<P>Yes I know you are going to say that Dennis is running a t-3 with a 3.4 and is making about 300 hp.  Well I doubt that and if he is it is a super inneficient 300 hp.  His turbo is way too small for the flow of a 3.4 engine.  his turbine will be spinning so much rpm and his compressor will produce a lot of heat moving that air.  Not taking anything away from Dennis as he did a great job with his Fiero and turbo install.  Its just that he could get a lot more from a 60-1 running 10 psi then from a t-3 running 10 psi.  But perhaps Dennis doesn't get on it much and never gets over 5 psi.  Don't know since I don't know his driving habits.<P>If you want to figure out which turbo is best for your application then go to <A HREF="http://www.turboneticsinc.com" TARGET=_blank>www.turboneticsinc.com</A>  and plot your own turbo graphs to see what would be best for your combo and driving style.<P>But I personally think a super 60-1 is the best overall for anything larger then a 2.8<P>------------------<BR>SOLD 85 GT w/ nitrous.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000010||DKOV||12-12-2001||09:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by aleroboy:<BR><B>If you go turbo the later style head on the 3.4L are the best. the 3.4L that comes in my car produces 170hp and 200ft/lbs stock!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Of course, the 3.4L DOHC pumps out 220 HP and 215 f.lbs. of Torque.  All this is stock, normally aspirated, raw HP.<P>With mods, 300+, still NO turbo.<P>I still can't figure why, with this motor floating around, anyone would want to Turbo a 2.8 motor.  Not that it isn't cool, but for the money, a 300 HP 3.4L DOHC can be had and installed.  Well... for what Predator spent  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||12-12-2001||11:04 PM||||aleroboy,<BR>you 170hp stock is at the flywheel, that's about 130hp at the wheels.  His 169 at the wheels is about 215hp gross at the flywheel and will probably hit 230 once his a/f ratio is squared away<P>DVROK,<BR>   yeah the DOHC makes 220 hp but at 6200rpm...  Preadator should be making that power at 5000rpm...and he can still turn up the boost and if his bottem end is well-balanced, he can spin it to 6200rpm as well.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DKOV||12-13-2001||01:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>DVROK,<BR>   yeah the DOHC makes 220 hp but at 6200rpm...  Preadator should be making that power at 5000rpm...and he can still turn up the boost and if his bottem end is well-balanced, he can spin it to 6200rpm as well.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't understand your point... the DOHC  revs out to 7500 rpms and does NOT require balancing.  The 220 HP figure is also bone stock, but who keeps their motor bone stock?  My DOHC is, on paper, at 322.8 HP although I have not got it to the Dyno yet.<P>I guess the bottom line is, whether your getting 300 HP from a normally aspirated DOHC or your getting 270 from a Turbocharged 2.8, isn't that enough?<P>NEVER  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||lou_dias||12-13-2001||09:13 AM||||most 2.8 fieros are not internally balanced, just the 88's that's another reason they got rid of the rev limiter in 88.<P>Hey Cooter got an etra 36hp on his 3.4 pushrod with that funky intake but it takes planning and design not just what's on paper.   Yeah 300hp is doable on the dohc but did you listen to the drivebility issues it has off idle and to 3000rpm?<P>A turbo car will run cleanly almost stock until the boost kicks in...no idling or drivability issues...||||reg||1
Z||000014||Turbo88||12-13-2001||11:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B> Of course, the 3.4L DOHC pumps out 220 HP and 215 f.lbs. of Torque.  All this is stock, normally aspirated, raw HP.<P>With mods, 300+, still NO turbo.<P>I still can't figure why, with this motor floating around, anyone would want to Turbo a 2.8 motor.  Not that it isn't cool, but for the money, a 300 HP 3.4L DOHC can be had and installed.  Well... for what Predator spent   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I totally agree except one point. The DOHC engines are pretty rare adn very expensive. If someone were looking for 300hp, they could get that out of a 3.4 turbo'ed for less money than just the DOHC engines. Don't get me wrong, my primary motivation for building a house with a three car garage is to have room to put in a DOHC motor! (sick yes I know). But most people aren't don't want to afford these oh so awsome engines. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||DKOV||12-13-2001||12:37 PM||||Is $1600 for a working motor with tranny (and only 26K miles) expensive?<P>I got mine for only $1000 with the factory 5-speed.<P>I've spend maybe another $1000 for the upgrades.  Maybe more.<P>Most Turbo kits start around $2500 - $3500 and that doesn't include build up on the motor to take a Turbo.<P>I suppose that the match works out to be about the same   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm not arguing, just trying to balance it in my mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000016||Turbo88||12-13-2001||12:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>Is $1600 for a working motor with tranny (and only 26K miles) expensive?<P>I got mine for only $1000 with the factory 5-speed.<P>I've spend maybe another $1000 for the upgrades.  Maybe more.<P>Most Turbo kits start around $2500 - $3500 and that doesn't include build up on the motor to take a Turbo.<P>I suppose that the match works out to be about the same    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm not arguing, just trying to balance it in my mind   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1600 for both the motor AND trans? Which trans?? WHERE TELLLL MEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE!!!!!!!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The best price I've found was 1500 for just the engine. I was figuring a 2500 dollar turbo kit on a stock 3.4 out of a 94 FBody (I can get them cheap here but I'm an office in the local FBody club. Guess that helps find people who know where to find cheap 3.4 pushrod engines.) There is a guy in our club that sprays his to hell and back and blows them all the time. He knows every salvage peice for 100 miles!<P>I have gotten to the point where I'm just going to buy a running car and yank the dohc out of it and toss the 2.8 back in and sell it. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||aleroboy||12-13-2001||01:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B> Of course, the 3.4L DOHC pumps out 220 HP and 215 f.lbs. of Torque. <BR>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>sorry I ment the pushrod motor.<BR>170hp and 200ft'lbs of torque.<BR>I don't like how cramed in the 3.4dohc is.||||reg||1
Z||000018||aleroboy||12-13-2001||01:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR>[B]aleroboy,<BR>you 170hp stock is at the flywheel, that's about 130hp at the wheels.  His 169 at the wheels is about 215hp gross at the flywheel and will probably hit 230 once his a/f ratio is squared away<BR>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>actually my car dyno's at 143.7 at the wheels with no mods. there are guys with basic bolt ons getting 160.<BR>still not much to brag about but I want to start with the 3.4 for my turbo set up<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||aleroboy||12-13-2001||01:33 PM||||I went to <A HREF="http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFmatch.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFmatch.html</A> <BR>and played arround with the turbo calculator.<BR>to get what i want out of my 3.4 I need a T04 or a T04s. I want to be able to run high levels of boost all the way to redline.<BR>I'm also going innercooled.||||reg||1
Z||000020||RedOktober||12-14-2001||08:24 PM||||Predator: I know what ur going through with your turbo set up. Mine has been a work in progress too. I've been trying to find ways to crank up the little IHI and not grenade the motor. <P>I dont feel as bad now that i am having as much trouble as you. <P>This is a message for all those who keep dreaming of free power and trying to do things on the cheap. DONT..... just wait until you know what you are doing, or spend the money and buy a kit. <P>I did the same as Predator and bought a used kit, and when I spent $750 Cnd to get my turbo rebuilt, I had some idiot tell me        (quoting GT Bastard)<BR>"Yeah, if you paid 750 to have a turbo rebuilt and "several hundred" for a crossover pipe, you're an idiot.... You might as well bend over."<P>All I have to say about these so called experts is: Talk to me when you've actually done one!<P>Turbo88<BR> Whats the status of your new kit .. ETA anytime soon??<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red6[1]a.jpg">  <BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||12-14-2001||09:29 PM||||I can understand turboing a 2.8/3.4L rather than installing a DOHC. There's nothing like the shove of the hand in the back you get from boost. I love the DOHC but for the effort and hassles, there is a certain amount of charm to the turbo. <P>Maybe it's the challenge too. <P>But the DOHC is sweet!<P>TK<P>||||reg||1
